```rust
pub fn put(&mut self, key: K, value: V) -> Option<V> {
    if let Some(&node) = self.map.get(&key) {
        let old = std::mem::replace(&mut self.nodes[node].value, value); // need placeholder...
    }
}
```

std::mem::replace requires Default for V. Alternative: use swap with a placeholder via `std::mem::take` (needs Default). V is not Default. So we need another approach:

```rust
if let Some(&node) = self.map.get(&key) {
    let old = std::mem::replace(&mut self.nodes[node].value, value);
```
This needs Default. No good.

Alternative: swap with a dummy? We can't construct a dummy V.

Use `ptr::read`/`write`? That's ugly. Better: restructure to store the value in a separate Vec, or use `Option<V>` in the node? Or just:

```rust
let node = ...;
let old_value = self.remove_value(node) ...
```

Actually, the simplest: keep the value in the node, but for replacement do:

```rust
// Swap the key/value pointers:
std::mem::swap(&mut self.nodes[node].value, &value_tmp)
```
We don't have a tmp V.

Alternative: since we're going to overwrite, use raw pointer:
```rust
let old = unsafe { std::ptr::read(&self.nodes[node].value) };
std::ptr::write(&mut self.nodes[node].value, value);
self.move_to_front(node);
Some(old)
```
This is technically sound (read is a move, then write initializes), but uses unsafe. Hmm.

Cleaner: store the value in the map's value slot instead of the node. That is, `HashMap<K, Slot>`, where `Slot = { node: usize, value: V }`. The linked list only holds node indices and prev/next. Then:

- get: find the slot's node index, move to front, return &slot.value via another map lookup.
- put existing: `let slot = self.map.get_mut(&key).unwrap(); std::mem::swap(&mut slot.value, &value)` — swap works without Default! Then return Some(value) (the swapped-out old value).

Yes! `std::mem::swap` requires neither Default nor Clone.

So:
```rust
pub fn put(&mut self, key: K, value: V) -> Option<V> {
    if let Some(slot) = self.map.get_mut(&key) {
        let old = std::mem::replace(...) // still needs Default
```
No — swap:
```rust
    if let Some(slot) = self.map.get_mut(&key) {
        std::mem::swap(&mut slot.value, &value);
        // value now holds the old one
        self.move_to_front(slot.node);
        return Some(value);
    }
    // New key
    if self.map.len() >= self.capacity {
        // Evict LRU
        let (k, slot) = self.remove_lru_node(); // pop the tail
        // slot is freed
    }
    // Allocate node
    ...
}
```

Wait, but K needs to be Clone for… if the value is in the map, the node doesn't need the key. Then iter needs the key. iter walks the linked list (nodes) and needs to look up the key by node index — O(n) reverse lookup per element → O(n²). No good.

So the key must be stored somewhere accessible per-node. Options:
1. Store the key in the node (K: Clone is available!). Then map: K -> (node index), and the node holds a clone of the key + value.
2. Store in the map: K -> node index, and have the node store a clone of the key.

In case 1: iter walks nodes and yields (&node.key, &node.value). put existing: swap node.value, move to front, return old value.

Borrowing for put existing:
```rust
if let Some(&node) = self.map.get(&key) {
    // Swap value
    let old = std::mem::replace(&mut self.nodes[node].value, value); // needs Default
```
No good. Use swap with a local variable:
```rust
let mut tmp = value;
std::mem::swap(&mut tmp, &mut self.nodes[node].value);
self.move_to_front(node);
Some(tmp)
```
This works without Default.

Actually simpler: just do `let old = std::mem::replace(&mut self.nodes[node].value, value);` — needs Default. The swap trick avoids that.

OK, let's define the whole structure:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::hash::Hash;

pub struct LruCache<K, V> {
    map: HashMap<K, usize>,
    nodes: Vec<Node<K, V>>,
    free: Vec<usize>,
    head: Option<usize>, // MRU
    tail: Option<usize>, // LRU
    capacity: usize,
}

struct Node<K, V> {
    key: K,
    value: V,
    prev: Option<usize>,
    next: Option<usize>,
}
```

Methods:

new(capacity): assert/panic if capacity == 0.
```rust
pub fn new(capacity: usize) -> Self {
    assert!(capacity > 0, "capacity must be greater than zero");
    LruCache { map: HashMap::new(), nodes: Vec::new(), free: Vec::new(), head: None, tail: None, capacity }
}
```

alloc_node:
```rust
fn alloc(&mut self, key: K, value: V) -> usize {
    let idx = match self.free.pop() {
        Some(i) => i,
        None => { self.nodes.push(Node { key, value, prev: None, next: None }); self.nodes.len() - 1 }
    };
    // If reusing from free, node exists with old contents; overwrite
    self.nodes[idx].key = key; // need key: move, but key is K: Clone...
}
```

Hmm, when reusing a freed node, the node still contains the old key/value (not dropped — when freeing, we should drop the value to avoid a leak). Let's drop the contents when freeing:

```rust
fn free_node(&mut self, i: usize) {
    // Drop key and value
    let node = self.nodes[i].take()? // Node<K,V> doesn't have take...
}
```

Node doesn't have Default, and take() needs Default. Alternative: leave the data in the node on free and just not drop it (memory retained until overwritten). This is a known trade-off; but it's cleaner to actually drop.

Alternative approach: store `Option<V>` in the node, drop on free with `take()`. But the value in iter: `Some(v)` — iter yields `&V` — `node.value.as_ref()`. And get returns `node.value.as_ref()`. This works and is clean:

```rust
struct Node<K, V> {
    key: K,
    value: Option<V>,
    prev: Option<usize>,
    next: Option<usize>,
}
```

- On free: `self.nodes[i].value.take()` (drops it), key remains (K: Clone, can't drop without Default… hmm, the key stays; a minor memory retention, but fine).
- On alloc: `self.nodes[i].value = Some(value)`, `self.nodes[i].key = key`.

Wait, key: when reusing a freed node, the old key remains in memory (not dropped). This is a minor leak of memory but acceptable. Alternatively use Option<K>… K: Clone but no Default; `Option<K>`'s take works! Option doesn't require Default for take. `self.nodes[i].key.take()` — yes, Option::take works on any T. So:

```rust
struct Node<K, V> {
    key: Option<K>,
    value: Option<V>,
    prev: Option<usize>,
    next: Option<usize>,
}
```

Clean. On free, drop both. On alloc, set both.

Hmm, but iter returns (&K, &V) — from node.key.as_ref(), node.value.as_ref(). get returns &V from node.value.as_ref().

Actually, hmm, is there a simpler classic design? Many implementations use a Vec-based list with indices and a free list, storing the value directly. Let's just go with the Option approach — it's sound.

Actually wait. Let me reconsider: do we really need the free list? Alternative: `Vec<Option<Node>>` — when removing, take the node out (drop it), leaving None holes. Allocation: pop from a free list of indices with holes, or push a new one. Same thing. The free Vec<usize> + Option field is fine.

Even simpler: since we only need O(1) amortized, we can keep the free list of node indices as described.

Now let's write all the operations:

```rust
impl<K: Hash + Eq + Clone, V> LruCache<K, V> {
    pub fn new(capacity: usize) -> Self {
        assert!(capacity != 0, "LruCache capacity must not be zero");
        Self {
            map: HashMap::new(),
            nodes: Vec::new(),
            free: Vec::new(),
            head: None,
            tail: None,
            capacity,
        }
    }

    fn alloc_node(&mut self, key: K, value: V) -> usize {
        let idx = match self.free.pop() {
            Some(idx) => {
                self.nodes[idx].key = Some(key);
                self.nodes[idx].value = Some(value);
                self.nodes[idx].prev = None;
                self.nodes[idx].next = None;
                idx
            }
            None => {
                let idx = self.nodes.len();
                self.nodes.push(Node { key: Some(key), value: Some(value), prev: None, next: None });
                idx
            }
        };
        idx
    }

    fn free_node(&mut self, idx: usize) {
        self.nodes[idx].key = None;
        self.nodes[idx].value = None;
        self.nodes[idx].prev = None;
        self.nodes[idx].next = None;
        self.free.push(idx);
    }
```

Wait — `self.nodes[idx].value = None;` — this moves None in, dropping the old Some(v). Yes, assignment drops the previous value. Good.

```rust
    fn link_front(&mut self, idx: usize) {
        let head = self.head;
        self.nodes[idx].prev = None;
        self.nodes[idx].next = head;
        if let Some(h) = head {
            self.nodes[h].prev = Some(idx);
        }
        self.head = Some(idx);
        if self.tail.is_none() {
            self.tail = Some(idx);
        }
    }
```

Hmm, be careful when linking a node that's already at the head: idx == head. In that case, next = head = Some(idx) — self-reference! We only call link_front after unlink, or on a fresh node, so it should be fine. But for safety, unlink first. Let's guarantee that.

```rust
    fn unlink(&mut self, idx: usize) {
        let prev = self.nodes[idx].prev.take();
        let next = self.nodes[idx].next.take();
        if let Some(p) = prev {
            self.nodes[p].next = next;
        }
        if let Some(n) = next {
            self.nodes[n].prev = prev;
        }
        if self.head == Some(idx) {
            self.head = next;
        }
        if self.tail == Some(idx) {
            self.tail = prev;
        }
    }

    fn move_to_front(&mut self, idx: usize) {
        if self.head == Some(idx) {
            return; // already MRU
        }
        self.unlink(idx);
        self.link_front(idx);
    }
```

Hmm wait, there's a subtle issue with unlink when the node is at the head: head = next. If the node is the only node: prev=None, next=None → head=None, tail=None. Good.

get:
```rust
    pub fn get(&mut self, key: &K) -> Option<&V> {
        let node = *self.map.get(key)?;
        self.move_to_front(node);
        self.nodes[node].value.as_ref()
    }
```
Borrowing: `self.map.get(key)?` — `?` inside a function returning Option. `*` copies the usize. The immutable borrow of self.map ends before move_to_front. NLL should handle this.

get_mut:
```rust
    pub fn get_mut(&mut self, key: &K) -> Option<&mut V> {
        let node = *self.map.get(key)?;
        self.move_to_front(node);
        self.nodes[node].value.as_mut()
    }
```

peek:
```rust
    pub fn peek(&self, key: &K) -> Option<&V> {
        self.map.get(key).and_then(|&node| self.nodes[node].value.as_ref())
    }
```

put:
```rust
    pub fn put(&mut self, key: K, value: V) -> Option<V> {
        if let Some(&node) = self.map.get(&key) {
            let old = self.nodes[node].value.take().expect("node value is present");
            self.nodes[node].value = Some(value);
            self.move_to_front(node);
            return Some(old);
        }
        // New key: possibly evict
        if self.map.len() >= self.capacity {
            self.evict_lru(); // removes tail entry from map and list, frees the node
        }
        let node = self.alloc_node(key, value);
        self.map.insert(...)  // key was moved into the node...
    }
```

Problem: key is moved into the node via alloc_node, but we also need key for the map's insert. K: Clone! So clone the key for the map:

```rust
    pub fn put(&mut self, key: K, value: V) -> Option<V> {
        if let Some(&node) = self.map.get(&key) {
            let old = self.nodes[node].value.take().expect("entry values are always present");
            self.nodes[node].value = Some(value);
            self.move_to_front(node);
            return Some(old);
        }
        if self.map.len() >= self.capacity {
            self.evict_lru();
        }
        let node = self.alloc_node(key.clone(), value);
        self.map.insert(key, node);
        self.link_front(node);
        None
    }
```

Wait, ordering: alloc_node, then map.insert, then link_front. Fine.

Hmm, `self.map.len() >= self.capacity` — since len never exceeds capacity, `>=` and `==` are equivalent. Use `==`? `>=` is safer.

evict_lru:
```rust
    fn evict_lru(&mut self) {
        let Some(tail) = self.tail else { return };
        let key = self.nodes[tail].key.take().expect("...");
        self.unlink(tail);
        self.free_node(tail);
        self.map.remove(&key);
    }
```

Note: `let Some(tail) = self.tail else` — let-else requires Rust 2021 / 1.65+. Fine, edition is 2021.

remove:
```rust
    pub fn remove(&mut self, key: &K) -> Option<V> {
        let node = match self.map.remove_entry(key) { Some((_, n)) => n, None => return None };
        let value = self.nodes[node].value.take()?; // expect
        self.unlink(node);
        self.free_node(node);
        Some(value)
    }
```

Order: map.remove_entry first, then take the value, then unlink, then free. Fine.

pop_lru:
```rust
    pub fn pop_lru(&mut self) -> Option<(K, V)> {
        let tail = self.tail?;
        let key = self.nodes[tail].key.take().expect("...");
        let value = self.nodes[tail].value.take().expect("...");
        self.unlink(tail);
        self.free_node(tail);
        self.map.remove(&key);
        Some((key, value))
    }
```

contains: `self.map.contains_key(key)`.
len: `self.map.len()`.
is_empty: `self.map.is_empty()`.
capacity: `self.capacity`.

resize:
```rust
    pub fn resize(&mut self, capacity: usize) {
        assert!(capacity != 0, "LruCache capacity must not be zero");
        self.capacity = capacity;
        while self.map.len() > capacity {
            self.evict_lru();
        }
    }
```

clear:
```rust
    pub fn clear(&mut self) {
        self.map.clear();
        // Drop all node contents
        for node in &mut self.nodes {
            node.key = None;
            node.value = None;
            node.prev = None;
            node.next = None;
        }
        // Rebuild free list to include all indices
        self.free.clear();
        for i in 0..self.nodes.len() {
            self.free.push(i);
        }
        self.head = None;
        self.tail = None;
    }
```

Hmm wait, when freeing, we push to self.free, but here the nodes still have their contents; we clear the contents and then push all the indices. Alternatively, we could just `self.nodes.clear()` and `self.free.clear()` — drop everything, and subsequent allocs will push new nodes. Simpler! clear just resets the state:

```rust
    pub fn clear(&mut self) {
        self.map.clear();
        self.nodes.clear();
        self.free.clear();
        self.head = None;
        self.tail = None;
    }
```

Yes — much cleaner. Drop all nodes (key+value get dropped).

iter:
```rust
    pub fn iter(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = (&K, &V)> + '_ {
        self.nodes
            .iter()
            .enumerate()
            ...
    }
```

Walk from head to tail:
```rust
    pub fn iter(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = (&K, &V)> + '_ {
        struct Iter<'a, K, V> {
            nodes: &'a Vec<Node<K, V>>,
            cur: Option<usize>,
        }
        impl<'a, K, V> Iterator for Iter<'a, K, V> {
            type Item = (&'a K, &'a V);
            fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {
                let idx = self.cur?;
                let node = &self.nodes[idx];
                let item = (node.key.as_ref()?, node.value.as_ref()?);
                self.cur = node.next;
                Some(item)
            }
        }
        Iter { nodes: &self.nodes, cur: self.head }
    }
